course: dictionary
lesson: 1948
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Look at the words around the word and ask: what makes sense here?
explain: «The Commonwealth Games is a big sports event every four years.» Sport and every four years are clues. A commonwealth here is a group of countries that take part together, not a type of wealth.
explain: Capital letters can help too. «The Commonwealth of Australia» is the name of a country, so the word is a name. «A commonwealth is a group of people or a nation working together for the good of all» is a general meaning, not a name. Check the sentence to see which one fits.
fact: A commonwealth is a group of people or a nation working together for the good of all.
fact: The Commonwealth is a group of more than fifty countries, most of which were once part of the British Empire, that work together on trade, education and sport.
fact: «The Commonwealth of Australia» is a name for the country, formed when the colonies joined in 1901.
fact: In English history, the Commonwealth was the time after King Charles I was executed, when England had no king and Oliver Cromwell ruled.
fact: Commonwealth Day and the Commonwealth Games are events for Commonwealth countries.
exercise: «Gold was found in Australia in the 1850s and people rushed there from all over the world, and in 1901 the colonies joined as the Commonwealth of Australia.» What is the Commonwealth of Australia? | A type of sport | A gold mine | A city full of visitors from the 1850s | *A name for the country formed when the colonies joined
exercise: A commonwealth is a group of people or a nation working together for the good of all. What does this mean? | A person who counts money | A type of coin | A country with no people in it | *A group working together for the good of all
exercise: «The Commonwealth of Nations is a voluntary group of 56 countries, most of which were once part of the British Empire, though some members, such as Rwanda and Mozambique, never were.» Which member never was part of the British Empire? | Canada | Australia | *Mozambique | India
test: «Usually only citizens can vote for a country's government, but there are exceptions.» What does exception mean here? | A type of vote | *Cases that do not follow the usual rule | Rules that always work | A country's new government
test: Which two US states are called commonwealths? | California and Nevada | Texas and Florida | Ohio and Maine | *Massachusetts and Virginia
exam: In English history, what was the Commonwealth? | A famous sports event | *The time after King Charles I was executed, when England had no king | A king who ruled England for fifty years | The time when the British Empire began
